<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carlos Finlay</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1833">1833</a>–<a href="/years/1915">1915</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Carlos Juan Finlay</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carlos Finlay was a Cuban physician and epidemiologist who in 1881 proposed that yellow fever was transmitted by a mosquito, now known as Aedes aegypti. His theory was confirmed in 1900 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Army commission led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-reed">Walter Reed</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/occupation-of-japan">American occupation</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, and the mosquito campaigns that followed eliminated yellow fever from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a>. He later served as Cuba's chief health officer.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carlos-finlay.jpg" alt="Carlos Finlay" width="320" height="467" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Finlay_Carlos_1833-1915.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
